
Workstation architecture for multi-agent orchestration and hybrid local inference
Engineering document

28 October 2026

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM


1. Purpose and epistemic status

This blueprint specifies a single compute node designed to orchestrate more than twenty artificial-intelligence agents, run language models locally, delegate part of the workload to external APIs, and preserve reproducible research results.

Epistemic status. This is a design specification, not an experimental report. No prototype has been assembled, no component has been purchased, no benchmark has been executed on the configurations described here. Every numerical value labelled specification is either a vendor-documented figure or a closed-form calculation whose inputs are stated. Every performance figure labelled target or acceptance threshold is an objective to be verified during the benchmark phase. Where the document states a conclusion about feasibility, that conclusion follows from arithmetic on documented quantities, not from an observed run. It must be confirmed by measurement before it justifies a purchase.

The document is organised around three candidate tiers, a mandatory platform burn-in, an expanded benchmark protocol covering dense, MoE and hybrid model classes, and a buy-versus-API cost rule. The discipline is explicit: rent capacity, benchmark, then purchase one GPU and expand only what measurement justifies.

---

2. Mathematical foundations

Four closed-form models govern the choice of hardware and software. None substitutes for measurement; each bounds what measurement must confirm.

2.1 Memory model

The video memory required for inference decomposes into four terms:

M_{\text{VRAM}} = M_{\text{weights}} + M_{\text{KV}} + M_{\text{activations}} + M_{\text{framework}}

Weights. For $N$ parameters quantized at $b$ bits:

M_{\text{weights}} = \frac{N \cdot b}{8 \times 10^9} \text{ GB}

A 32-billion-parameter dense model at 4 bits occupies ~16 GB of raw weights, ~17–18 GB after metadata. A 70-billion-parameter dense model at 4 bits occupies ~35 GB; at FP8, ~70 GB.

Sparse MoE clarification. For a mixture-of-experts model, the total parameter count governs storage, but the active parameter count governs decode bandwidth. GPT-OSS-120B has 117B total parameters and 5.1B active parameters per token (128 experts, top-4 routing), which is why it can decode interactively on hardware that cannot hold a dense 117B model at usable speed.

KV cache — corrected formula, with batch explicit.

M_{\text{KV}} = 2 \cdot L \cdot H_{kv} \cdot d \cdot S \cdot B \cdot \frac{b_{kv}}{8 \times 10^9} \text{ GB}

For a Qwen3-32B-class model (64 layers, 8 KV heads, $d=128$) at FP16, the per-token cost is 256 KiB. A 32 GB card at gpu_memory_utilization=0.90 leaves roughly 9–10 GB for KV after weights and framework overhead. That is approximately 35–40K tokens at FP16 and 70–80K tokens at FP8. A dozen concurrent requests at 4K–32K will not fit without preemption or queuing. The acceptance criterion is therefore measured KV pool capacity and preemption rate, not a Little's-law average $L$.

KV precision policy — three tiers.

Engine role KV precision Precondition
Critic / reasoning engine BF16 until it passes the SI TEAM reasoning test under FP8; then FP8 Mandatory quality test on critical-analysis and documentary-research tasks, not a generic needle-in-a-haystack
Generalist engine (above 8K context) FP8 Passes retrieval and summarisation tests at FP8 on sm_120
Batch documentary synthesis FP8 (default) Human review before result registry ingestion

The FP8 accuracy evidence that drove an earlier, more conservative policy was measured on Hopper GPUs with FlashAttention-3 at 128K context, where a kernel-level accumulation issue collapsed needle-in-a-haystack accuracy from 91% to 13% before a fix restored it to 89%. That issue was architecture- and context-specific. On sm_120 (consumer Blackwell), FP8 KV must be tested directly at 4K and 32K on the SI TEAM tasks; extrapolation from Hopper results is not sufficient evidence either to reject or to accept FP8.

TurboQuant is not a recommended lever. The vLLM study concludes that FP8 remains the default choice; aggressive 4-bit KV quantization extends capacity to 2.3–3.7× but incurs a 40–52% throughput reduction, and lower KV storage cost does not translate into faster serving. TurboQuant is an experiment to be measured, not a production policy.

2.2 Concurrency model

Little's law remains the dimensioning tool: $L = \lambda \cdot W$. The reference scenario is a design hypothesis, not a measured distribution:

Task type Share Target $\lambda$ (req/s/agent) Expected $W$ Implied $L$ (20 agents)
Summary (4K) 40% 0.10 1–2 s 2–4
Critical analysis (8K) 30% 0.05 4–6 s 4–6
Documentary research (32K) 20% 0.01 15–30 s 3–6
Script generation 10% 0.02 8–12 s 3–5

The benchmark must record real $\lambda$ and $W$ per task type and recompute $L$ from those measurements, with separate service-time profiles for dense and MoE engines.

2.3 Roofline — pedagogical only

Autoregressive generation is memory-bound:

\text{Tok/s}_{\max} \approx \frac{\beta_{\text{VRAM}}}{M_{\text{weights, active}} + B \cdot m_{\text{KV per token}}}

For a sparse MoE, the denominator uses active weight bytes, not total weight bytes. This is why GPT-OSS-120B can decode at tens of tokens per second on hardware whose bandwidth would make a dense 117B model unusable. The roofline explains behaviour; it does not appear in acceptance thresholds.

2.4 Parallelism and Amdahl's law

Amdahl's bound applies to tensor parallelism of a single model. It does not apply to Mode B, where each GPU runs an independent engine. Without NVLink, inter-GPU communication transits PCIe, reducing the parallelisable fraction; two GPUs do not form a GPU of double capacity. Mode B (independent models) is preferred over shared tensor parallelism until a measured gain justifies the complexity.

