WRX90 is not plug-and-play. Documented issues include crashes after extended operation, memory modules not recognised by monitoring tools, PCIe bus drops ("GPU has fallen off the bus"), and boot failures after BIOS updates. The IPMI requirement is a recovery mechanism, and the 72-hour platform burn-in of §3.4 is mandatory before any inference benchmark begins.

3.3 Power and thermal

Tier V (two 3090s). Peak draw ≈ 1,080–1,200 W. A 1,200 W ATX 3.1 unit is adequate; 1,400 W is preferred for transient headroom.

Tier R (one 5090). Peak draw ≈ 955–1,075 W. A 1,200 W ATX 3.1 unit with a native 12V-2x6 connector is specified. ATX 3.1 requires the PSU to sustain 200% of rated power for 100 µs (the 1 ms allowance is 180%). This is the transient specification the PSU must meet, not a 200% / 1 ms figure.

Tier C (dual 5090, Mode B). Each 5090 is power-limited to 450 W, not 575 W. Measured testing shows ~5% gaming performance loss at 450 W and ~10% loss at 400 W. A 5% loss is an easy trade for reduced connector current and thermal stress. The benchmark must measure the inference throughput loss at the reference batch sizes; if the loss exceeds 15%, the 450 W cap is reconsidered; if it exceeds 25%, dual-5090 Mode B is rejected.

Mode B electrical limit. Dual 5090s at 450 W plus a 350 W CPU and system overhead draw ~1,450–1,550 W at the wall. A 120 V / 15 A circuit is limited to ~1,440 W continuous, so Mode B requires either a 240 V circuit or a dedicated 20 A circuit. A 1,500 VA UPS is insufficient; the UPS must be sized to the measured peak. The PSU is 2,000 W ATX 3.1, or dual 1,000 W units with two independent 12V-2x6 circuits and no Y-cable.

Thermal acceptance. Dual-GPU load for 30 minutes: GPU 0 and GPU 1 below 83 °C, no clock throttling above 5%, ambient below 25 °C. If the chassis cannot meet these thresholds with the chosen card spacing, Mode B is not mechanically feasible in that chassis.

3.4 Platform burn-in protocol — mandatory before inference benchmarking

Test Protocol Acceptance
Suspend/resume 20 cycles of suspend-to-RAM and resume No crash, no PCIe device loss, IPMI log clean
Memory stress memtest86+ or equivalent, minimum 4 full passes Zero errors across all eight channels
PCIe AER monitoring 24 h idle + 24 h light load, sample once per second No uncorrectable errors, no "GPU has fallen off the bus" events
ZFS scrub after simulated power loss Hard power cut, reboot, full scrub Zero checksum errors; pool imports cleanly
Thermal soak 8 h sustained combined CPU/GPU load GPU < 83 °C, throttle < 5%, no shutdown

A node that passes a 24-hour inference run but crashes on the third suspend/resume cycle is not a reliable orchestration node.

3.5 Software execution architecture

The six service layers are retained: orchestrator (durable state in PostgreSQL), bounded task queue (priorities, quotas, deadlines, backpressure — an unbounded queue is prohibited), local inference gateway (OpenAI-compatible, one engine per GPU, gpu_memory_utilization set explicitly), external API gateway (centralised keys, quotas, costs), result registry (sources, model versions, parameters, fact/inference/hypothesis), and observability (Prometheus, Grafana, structured logs).

Mandatory metrics. TTFT p50/p95/p99, TPOT p50/p95/p99, tokens/s per agent, queue rejection rate, KV pool size, preemption rate, VRAM utilisation, system RAM utilisation, Wh per successful task, cost per successful task, error count by category.

Prefix cache isolation. Prefix caching is enabled only for the verified common prefix (system prompt and tool definitions), never for user content. cache_salt must be populated with a distinct tenant- or security-context-specific salt in every chat completion request; vLLM partitions the prefix cache so that only requests carrying the same salt can share cached blocks. A known vulnerability class involves cache salts being dropped during tool continuations, defeating tenant isolation; the gateway must enforce salt presence and pin the vLLM image version to one where the fix is applied.

Engine selection. vLLM and SGLang are both benchmarked on the 40/30/20/10 reference scenario at 20 concurrent agents. SGLang's prefix-cache advantage is workload-shape dependent: it cuts TTFT from 440 ms to 38 ms on multi-turn agent traffic with an 8,000-token system context, but ties vLLM on unique prompts. The selection rule is measured TTFT p95 under the actual workload: SGLang is selected if it reduces TTFT p95 by more than 20% at equal or better task success; vLLM is selected if the gain is below 10% or if model support is the deciding constraint. Between 10% and 20%, vLLM is preferred for operational simplicity.

Quantization. W4A16 (4-bit weight-only) offers the best cost-efficiency for synchronous deployments and is competitive with 8-bit formats on accuracy, with low single-digit degradation on standard benchmarks. FP8 W8A8 is lossless across model scales and is the preferred format where memory permits. NVFP4 is the format of choice for MoE models with native hardware support; GPT-OSS-120B in NVFP4 fits in ~57 GB and decodes at 31.4 tok/s at concurrency 1 on the DGX Spark.

