6. Principles and constraints

P1 — Measure before investing. No component is added without a benchmark demonstrating that it lifts an identified bottleneck. This is binding on CPU core count, RAM capacity, the second GPU, KV precision, and the power cap.

P2 — Separate orchestration from inference. The agent scheduler and the inference scheduler are distinct. A GPU, a model, or an API provider can be replaced without rebuilding the architecture.

P3 — Do not confuse cumulative capacity with shared capacity. Two GPUs do not form a double-capacity GPU. Mode B (independent engines) is preferred over tensor parallelism until a gain is measured.

P4 — Bound queues and budgets. Every task has a time, token and spending cap. Every queue is bounded. Backpressure is explicit. An unbounded system saturates RAM or empties an API budget.

P5 — Distinguish fact, inference and hypothesis. The result registry traces every conclusion to its source, model version and parameters. A conclusion is not evidence because an agent copied it into a report.

Non-negotiable constraints.

· No purchase before 72-hour platform burn-in.
· No dual-5090 Mode B before the 450 W power-cap test.
· No FP8 KV on the critic engine without a task-specific reasoning test on sm_120.
· No DGX Spark or Strix Halo in an interactive path.
· No Tier C component before Tier V and Tier R have failed a specific threshold.
· cache_salt is mandatory on every request; prefix caching is never shared across confidential content.
· Backups are validated by a real restore, not by the existence of a backup job.
· Mode B requires a 240 V or dedicated 20 A circuit; a 1,500 VA UPS is insufficient.

