Appendix A — Platform topology and PCIe lane allocation

The WRX90 WS EVO exposes seven physical PCIe 5.0 x16 slots, but only one is wired at x8. The remaining six are full x16 from the CPU. This asymmetry determines which slots may host a primary inference engine.

Slot Wiring Source Permitted use Prohibited use
Slot 7 PCIe 5.0 x16 CPU Primary GPU (GPU 0) —
Slot 5 PCIe 5.0 x16 CPU Secondary GPU (GPU 1, Mode B) —
Slot 4 PCIe 5.0 x16 CPU GPU reserve / high-speed NIC —
Slot 3 PCIe 5.0 x16 CPU GPU reserve / NVMe HBA —
Slot 2 PCIe 5.0 x16 CPU Future expansion —
Slot 1 PCIe 5.0 x16 CPU Future expansion —
Slot 6 PCIe 5.0 x16 physical, x8 electrical CPU Secondary x8 card only Primary GPU — never

The board also provides two MCIO connectors (PCIe 5.0 x4 or 4× SATA) and one SlimSAS SFF-8654 connector (PCIe 4.0 x4 or 4× SATA) for storage expansion. The MCIO and SlimSAS lane assignments must be checked against the lane-sharing matrix before populating any PCIe slot beyond Slots 7 and 5; a populated MCIO connector can disable or bifurcate an adjacent PCIe slot depending on the board revision.

Two further platform constraints are non-negotiable. First, the board supports only DDR5 RDIMM ECC; UDIMM modules are physically incompatible with the slot keying and will not POST. Second, the board requires the 2×6-pin GFX_12V1 or GFX_12V2 PCIe power connector to be populated in addition to the 24-pin ATX and the 2×8-pin CPU power connectors; omitting it leaves the PCIe slots without auxiliary rail capacity.

For Mode B, the recommendation is one GPU in Slot 7 and one in Slot 5. This places both cards on independent CPU root ports and avoids the x8 electrical limit of Slot 6. If a third GPU is later added for a dedicated batch engine, Slot 4 is the next full-x16 slot; Slot 6 should be reserved for a NIC or HBA.

