Appendix C — GPU power delivery and connector design

C.1 Transient behaviour of the RTX 5090

The RTX 5090 carries a 575 W sustained TDP, but its transient profile is the design driver, not the sustained figure. Independent measurement records excursions of 627.5 W for 10–20 ms, 738.2 W for 5–10 ms, 823.6 W for 1–5 ms, and 901.1 W for under 1 ms. The 12V-2x6 connector's continuous rating is 600 W; the card is therefore operating at the connector's continuous limit while producing transients 50% above it.

C.2 ATX 3.1 transient specification

ATX 3.1 requires the PSU to sustain 200% of rated power for 100 µs and 180% for 1 ms. A 1,200 W ATX 3.1 unit therefore tolerates a 2,400 W excursion for 100 µs and a 2,160 W excursion for 1 ms. A single 5090's 901 W sub-millisecond spike is well inside that envelope. Two 5090s spiking simultaneously produce approximately 1,800 W, which is 150% of a 1,200 W unit's rating — still within the 1 ms allowance but with less margin. A 2,000 W ATX 3.1 unit for Mode B is sized to keep the simultaneous spike below 100% of nominal.

C.3 Connector current distribution

The 12V-2x6 connector uses 12 power pins (6 pairs of 12V wires) and 4 signal pins for current monitoring and temperature feedback. At 600 W and 12 V, the aggregate current is 50 A, or approximately 8.3 A per pin if perfectly balanced. Uneven contact resistance across pins is the documented failure mechanism in melting cases; the connector's per-pin current is not the problem, the distribution is.

Power-limiting each Mode B 5090 to 450 W reduces aggregate current to 37.5 A and per-pin current to approximately 6.25 A under balanced load. This is a 25% reduction in connector stress at a measured performance cost of ~5%. The 450 W cap is therefore the default Mode B configuration, not an optional experiment.

C.4 Mode A vs Mode B PSU specification

Mode GPU configuration PSU specification Rationale
A 1× 5090 at 575 W 1,200 W ATX 3.1, native 12V-2x6 Peak system draw ~955–1,075 W; 1,200 W provides 10–20% transient margin
B 2× 5090 at 450 W each 2,000 W ATX 3.1, two independent 12V-2x6 circuits Peak system draw ~1,450–1,550 W; 2,000 W keeps simultaneous spikes below nominal
B (alternative) 2× 5090 at 450 W Dual 1,000 W PSUs, two distinct 12V-2x6 circuits, no Y-cable Permitted only if each PSU is loaded below 85% at combined peak; a Y-cable defeats the purpose

A 1,200 W Mode A unit cannot serve Mode B. If Mode B is a realistic near-term target, the 2,000 W unit should be specified at build time to avoid a post-hoc PSU swap.

