Appendix D — Thermal design and dual-GPU chassis requirements

Two RTX 5090s at 575 W each dissipate 1,150 W into the chassis volume. The thermal failure mode is not gradual; it is throttling within the first hour of sustained inference.

Measured data from a production dual-5090 air-cooled workstation: both GPUs consistently hit 80–83 °C under sustained load, fans ramped to 2,800 RPM, and boost clocks sagged from 2,407 MHz to 2,200 MHz, a ~200 MHz reduction that produced inconsistent, asymmetric throttling between the two cards. A custom water loop reduced load temperatures to 52–55 °C, recovered 3–5% boost clock, and cut noise from 48 dB to 29 dB.

The SI TEAM thermal acceptance protocol is calibrated to this evidence:

Parameter Threshold Action if exceeded
GPU 0 / GPU 1 temperature under 30 min dual load < 83 °C Redesign airflow or adopt water cooling
Clock throttling < 5% Verify boost clock retention against rated spec
Ambient room temperature < 25 °C Facility cooling insufficient for the node
Thermal soak (8 h combined CPU/GPU) No shutdown, no throttle > 5% Chassis or cooling inadequate for Mode B

The 83 °C threshold is the air-cooling boundary, not the silicon limit. NVIDIA has raised the edge-throttle threshold on the 5090 to approximately 90 °C, but operating at the edge of the throttle curve produces the asymmetric behaviour that makes multi-GPU inference unpredictable. The 83 °C acceptance threshold preserves a 7 °C margin to the hardware limit.

Chassis requirement for Mode B. The chassis must accept two 3–4-slot cards with at least one free slot of spacing between them, and must provide direct airflow to the intake fans of the lower card. Cards mounted 10–15 mm above the chassis floor starve their intake fans and overheat regardless of case fan count. A full-tower chassis with a ventilated floor and a front-to-back airflow path is the minimum; a water-cooled configuration is the only measured path to sustained sub-60 °C operation.

