Appendix E — KV cache sizing calculations and model-specific parameters

E.1 Closed-form KV formula

The KV cache per token is:

m_{\text{KV per token}} = 2 \cdot L \cdot H_{kv} \cdot d \cdot \frac{b_{kv}}{8 \times 10^9} \text{ GB}

For a Qwen3-32B-class dense model with 64 layers, 8 KV heads, head dimension 128:

· FP16 KV: $2 \times 64 \times 8 \times 128 \times 2 = 262{,}144$ bytes = 256 KiB per token.
· FP8 KV: 128 KiB per token.
· 4K context, batch 1, FP16: 1 GB.
· 32K context, batch 1, FP16: 8 GB.
· 128K context, batch 1, FP16: 32 GB.

At gpu_memory_utilization=0.90 on a 32 GB card, the engine is permitted 28.8 GB. After 17–18 GB of 4-bit weights and 1–2 GB of framework and activation overhead, approximately 9–10 GB remains for KV. That budget supports:

Context length Batch at FP16 KV Batch at FP8 KV
4K ~8–10 ~16–20
8K ~4–5 ~8–10
32K ~1 ~2–3

These are theoretical ceilings. In practice, vLLM's paged KV allocator, block fragmentation, and preemption behaviour reduce the usable batch. The benchmark must record KV pool size and preemption rate as measured values, not infer them from the formula.

E.2 Active-parameter KV for sparse MoE

GPT-OSS-120B has 117B total parameters, 36 layers, 128 experts, 4 active experts per token, and ~5.1B active parameters. Its KV cache is computed from the layer count and KV head configuration of the attention stack, not from the expert count. The active-parameter advantage applies to weight reads per token, not to KV capacity. A 120B MoE therefore has a KV footprint comparable to a dense model of similar layer count and head dimension, while decoding faster because it reads only ~5.1B active parameters' worth of expert weights per step.

E.3 KV precision decision matrix

Engine role Precision Precondition Fallback
Critic / reasoning BF16 Passes SI TEAM reasoning test at FP8 on sm_120 within 2 pp of BF16 Remain BF16; reduce batch or context instead
Generalist (> 8K) FP8 Passes 4K and 32K needle tests on sm_120 Reduce context to 8K or offload long tasks to batch
Batch documentary FP8 Human review before registry ingestion Q4 only as an experiment with measured 40–52% throughput cost

TurboQuant and other aggressive KV quantization methods are not production levers. The measured throughput cost of 4-bit KV is 40–52%, and lower KV storage cost does not translate into faster serving. FP8 is the default; BF16 is the critic safeguard.

