Appendix F — Inference engine configuration parameters

F.1 vLLM parameters for the SI TEAM gateway

Parameter Value Rationale
gpu_memory_utilization 0.90 (explicit) Leaves 10% for CUDA context and framework; higher values risk OOM under fragmentation
enable_prefix_caching True Shared system prompt and tool definitions across 20 agents
cache_salt Mandatory per request Partitions prefix cache by tenant or security context
max_num_seqs Measured, not default Set to the measured KV-capacity batch ceiling, not the library default
max_model_len 32,768 for generalist; 8,192 for critic Prevents a single 128K request from monopolising the KV pool
enable_chunked_prefill True Prevents long prefill from blocking decode of in-flight requests
kv_cache_dtype fp8 (generalist), auto (critic = BF16) Per Appendix E.3
swap_space 0 or minimal CPU swap for KV is a latency cliff; bound the queue instead

cache_salt mechanics. The salt is injected into the hash of the first KV block, so only requests carrying the same salt can reuse cached blocks. The gateway must populate cache_salt with a distinct value per tenant or per security context, not a global constant. A known failure mode involves the salt being dropped on the pooling path when chunked preprocessing is enabled; the gateway must verify salt presence in logs and pin the vLLM image to a version where the fix is applied.

Chunked prefill interaction. In some vLLM versions, enable_chunked_prefill conflicts with enable_prefix_caching, and the server silently falls back to non-cached mode. The benchmark must verify that prefix caching remains active when chunked prefill is enabled; a silent fallback invalidates the TTFT comparison between engines.

F.2 SGLang parameters

SGLang uses RadixAttention for prefix caching and does not expose cache_salt in the same form. For the SI TEAM deployment, SGLang is a benchmark candidate only, not the default engine, because the tenant-isolation mechanism is less directly controlled. If SGLang wins the TTFT comparison by more than 20%, the gateway must implement prefix isolation at the routing layer — separate engine instances per security context — rather than relying on a salt parameter.

F.3 Engine selection rule

Measured TTFT p95 improvement (SGLang vs vLLM at 20 agents) Selection
> 20% and task success equal or better SGLang
10–20% vLLM (operational simplicity)
< 10% or task success worse vLLM

The rule is evaluated on the 70B-class and 120B-class models, not on an 8B proxy. The published 29% advantage of SGLang over vLLM narrows to single digits at 70B FP8; the threshold reflects that scale.

