Appendix G — Model class specifications for the benchmark matrix

Class Candidate Total params Active params Layers Attention Quantization VRAM footprint
Dense Qwen3-32B 32.8B 32.8B 64 GQA, 8 KV heads, dim 128 4-bit W4A16 ~17–18 GB
Sparse MoE GPT-OSS-120B 117B 5.1B 36 Alternating sliding-window / full attention Native MXFP4 ~57–64 GB
Hybrid / long-context Hybrid-attention 27B–35B class ~27–35B ~27–35B Varies Hybrid KV layout Model-dependent Measured

GPT-OSS-120B is the critical MoE candidate. Its native MXFP4 quantization means the MoE weights are trained at 4-bit precision, not post-hoc quantized; this preserves quality better than post-training 4-bit weight-only quantization of a dense model. Its 5.1B active parameters per forward pass mean that decode bandwidth is governed by ~3–4 GB of active expert weights, not the full 57–64 GB on-disk footprint. On hardware with 32 GB of VRAM, expert offloading to system RAM is required; the decode rate depends on the PCIe and system-memory bandwidth available for offloaded experts.

The hybrid-attention model is a risk probe, not a recommendation. Hybrid KV layouts (sliding-window plus full attention, or linear-attention layers interleaved with standard attention) do not obey the standard KV formula in Appendix E.1. Community reports of 35B-A3B hybrid models running 256K context on a single 5090 are anecdotal and unverified; the benchmark must measure whether stock vLLM or SGLang can execute the model's KV layout on sm_120 at all. If the software stack cannot run it, the class is excluded regardless of its theoretical capacity.

